# Swift & SwiftUI Bug Prevention Rules

This document captures patterns and anti-patterns discovered during development to prevent recurring bugs.

## 1. Naming Collisions with SwiftData Models

### Problem
Creating a struct with the same name as an existing SwiftData `@Model` class causes "ambiguous type lookup" errors.

### Example Bug
```swift
// SavedVerse.swift - existing SwiftData model
@Model
final class SavedVerse { ... }

// UserProfile.swift - accidentally created struct with same name
struct SavedVerse: Codable { ... }  // ❌ CONFLICT
```

### Prevention
- **Before creating any new type, search the codebase**: `grep -r "class TypeName\|struct TypeName" --include="*.swift"`
- **Use distinct naming for lightweight data transfer structs**: Prefix with purpose (e.g., `OnboardingSavedVerse`, `APIResponse`, `DTO`)
- **SwiftData models own their names**: Never reuse a SwiftData model name for structs

---

## 2. Duplicate Type Declarations Across Files

### Problem
Declaring the same type name in multiple files causes "Invalid redeclaration" compilation errors, even if the implementations differ.

### Example Bug
```swift
// OnboardingComponents.swift - shared component file
struct TypewriterText: View { ... }  // ✅ Shared component

// PrayerFlowView.swift - separate feature file
struct TypewriterText: View { ... }  // ❌ CONFLICT: Same name, different implementation
```

### Prevention
- **Before creating any new type, search for existing declarations**:
  ```bash
  grep -r "struct TypeName\|class TypeName\|enum TypeName" --include="*.swift"
  ```
- **For shared components**: Place in dedicated component files (e.g., `OnboardingComponents.swift`)
- **For feature-specific components**: Use descriptive prefixes (e.g., `PrayerTypewriterText`, `OnboardingTypewriterText`)
- **If types serve different purposes**: Rename one to reflect its specific context
- **When creating reusable components**: Check if a similar component already exists before creating a new one

### Resolution
If duplicate found:
1. Determine which is the "canonical" version (usually the one in shared component files)
2. Rename the duplicate with a context-specific prefix
3. Update all usages of the renamed type

